{{% author %}}By Yuan Zhou{{% /author %}} 

Chinese is written without spaces between words, unlike every language covered so far in this chapter. `tokens()` relies on the ICU library, mentioned in the [Overview page](/multilingual/overview), to find word boundaries as it segments the text. Once tokenised, cleaning up the result follows a familiar pattern: we use the [Marimo](https://github.com/koheiw/marimo) stopwords list (`stopwords("zh_cn", source = "marimo")`) and a minimum word length (`min_nchar = 2`) to remove function words, since many Chinese function words are a single character long. You can keep only Chinese characters with the Unicode script pattern `"^\\p{script=Hani}+$"`, which discards any stray Latin letters or digits ("Hani" refers to Han characters, the script shared by Chinese and, historically, Japanese).

```{r, message=FALSE}
library(quanteda)
library(quanteda.corpora)
```

```{r include=FALSE}
options(width = 110)
```

```{r}
corp <- corpus_reshape(data_corpus_udhr["cmn_hans"], to = "paragraphs")
toks <- tokens(corp, remove_punct = TRUE, remove_numbers = TRUE) |> 
  tokens_remove(pattern = stopwords("zh_cn", source = "marimo"), min_nchar = 2) |> 
  tokens_keep(pattern = "^\\p{script=Hani}+$", valuetype = 'regex')
print(toks[2], max_ndoc = 1, max_ntok = -1)
```

```{r}
# construct document-feature matrix
dfmat <- dfm(toks)
print(dfmat)
```
